("v4", "type", "String")
("v4", "scalar", "# SRP10 + SRP12 — external selectors and invariant robustness\n\n## Outcome\n\nThe OpenAI math intake sharpens two boundaries without changing the admitted Spin(2,3) position.\n\n1. The Haldane-chain boundary field and the Heisenberg vanishing-field construction are genuine **conditional selectors**. They demonstrate how an independently supplied field selects a state, a direction, and sometimes a topological sector. They do not derive the sign of that field from symmetric premises.\n2. The Laughlin result is a strong **carrier-specific stability theorem**. It does not transfer to the QWZ–Lindblad model in paper 3 merely because both settings use topological language.\n3. The entropy counterexample and the paper-3 dephasing model give two different warnings against topology-to-dynamics inference: homological growth can coexist with zero topological entropy, and a fixed band invariant can coexist with a continuously changing response.\n\nThe resulting position is:\n\n> **EXTERNAL-SELECTORS-CONDITIONAL / ORIGIN-STILL-UNFIXED / TOPOLOGY-DOES-NOT-DETERMINE-DYNAMICS.**\n\nThis is a source-scoped theorem and transfer audit. The source proofs and Lean artifacts were inspected but not independently rebuilt.\n\n## 1. Typed transfer discipline\n\nA mathematical result can enter the Spin(2,3) fact space in one of four ways:\n\n| Admission type | Required map | Permitted use |\n|---|---|---|\n| exact transfer | carrier, symmetry, hypotheses, conclusion, and quantifiers agree | scoped theorem |\n| proof template | proof architecture agrees but objects differ | new proof obligation |\n| countermodel | proposed implication is universal enough that a source-domain counterexample refutes it | rejection of the universal implication only |\n| analogy | vocabulary or qualitative pattern agrees | question generation only |\n\nFamilies 268, 269, and 271 are proof templates or source-domain examples here. Family 151 is a countermodel only to an unrestricted homology-growth-to-orbit-complexity implication. None is an exact theorem about the Spin(2,3) carrier.\n\n## 2. SRP10 selector tournament\n\n### 2.1 The MECE partition\n\nThe seven branches are classified by the earliest independently specified datum that is odd under the residual reversal:\n\n| Branch | What can happen | Selector verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| no source | invariant mixture or orbit | no sign |\n| infinitesimal bulk source | order of limits selects a broken-symmetry state | conditional source |\n| boundary field/condition | boundary data select a state or sector | conditional source |\n| topological label | labels/certifies an already selected sector | certificate, not cause |\n| initial-state asymmetry | dynamics transmits the initial sign | conditional source |\n| environment asymmetry | coupling transmits environmental sign | conditional source |\n| measurement/calibration | reported sign is fixed | label only |\n\nThese are mutually exclusive by provenance, not by later mathematical form. A boundary field that also changes a topological index remains in the boundary branch because the field is the earliest odd datum.\n\n### 2.2 Family 268: boundary field, gap, then index\n\nThe source studies an odd open spin-one antiferromagnetic Heisenberg chain with the same fixed positive magnetic field at both endpoints. At `h=3/5`, it proves a gap greater than `log(10)/392` for every chain parameter `L>=960`. The finite-volume ground line is unique. Every subsequential local limit is locally unique and gapped and has Tasaki index `-1`.\n\nThe logical order matters:\n\n```text\nchosen endpoint-field sign\n        ↓\nunique finite-volume ground state + uniform gap\n        ↓\nsubsequential boundary-selected infinite-volume state\n        ↓\ntopological index -1\n```\n\nThe index is therefore a certificate attached to the boundary-selected state in this construction. The result does not prove that the bulk symmetry generates the endpoint-field sign. Nor does it prove convergence or global uniqueness of the infinite-volume state. No typed map identifies the Tasaki index with the residual Spin(2,3) orientation.\n\n### 2.3 Family 271: magnitude after a direction-selecting limit\n\nThe source considers the isotropic nearest-neighbour quantum Heisenberg ferromagnet on `Z^d`, `d>=3`. It constructs finite-volume Gibbs states with a positive field\n\n`h_N = N^(-1/2)`\n\nand only then takes a subsequential thermodynamic limit. Although `h_N -> 0`, its volume contribution beats the boundary-order cost. The resulting zero-field KMS state has\n\n`omega(S_0^z) >= S/4`.\n\nThis cleanly separates direction and magnitude:\n\n- the positive `z` direction is specified by the source before the limit;\n- the theorem proves that the selected infinite-volume branch retains a nonzero magnitude;\n- rotating or reversing the source gives another branch;\n- averaging the rotation orbit can restore zero one-point magnetization.\n\nSpontaneous symmetry breaking therefore generates multiple ordered states. The order-of-limits prescription selects one; it is not an intrinsic sign-producing law.\n\n### 2.4 Anti-circularity test\n\nFor every proposed selector:\n\n1. identify the earliest odd datum;\n2. delete it and recompute the signed observable;\n3. reverse it and recompute;\n4. distinguish a change of physical state from a relabelling of the readout;\n5. record whether uniqueness is global or conditional on the datum.\n\nA genuine intrinsic derivation must retain a unique physical sign after deletion, or derive the datum from an earlier independently testable law. Both external examples fail that stronger test by design: they are successful conditional selectors.\n\n## 3. SRP12 robustness tournament\n\n### 3.1 MECE branches\n\n| Branch | Required evidence | Result here |\n|---|---|---|\n| invariant without gap | invariant only | insufficient for dynamics or stability |\n| uniform unperturbed gap | size-independent lower bound | family 269 Lean scope |\n| gap stable in a normed perturbation class | carrier + norm + threshold + uniform quantifier | family 269 paper theorem |\n| topological action without complexity | explicit countermodel | family 151 |\n| dynamical change without topological change | fixed invariant, changing generator/response | paper 3 itself |\n| finite-size artefact | convergence or exact bound absent | remains a risk for numerical cusp claims |\n\n### 3.2 Family 151: homology is not orbit complexity\n\nFamily 151 constructs a noninvertible `C^1` self-map of a compact smooth manifold with zero topological entropy even though the induced map on second real homology has eigenvalue `2`. The paper explicitly notes that the homological intertwining is not a dynamical semiconjugacy.\n\nThis refutes a universal inference from homological growth to orbit complexity in that class. It does **not** refute paper 2's winding calculation. It does block any later move from “nonzero topological data” directly to “complex, dissipative, chaotic, or irreversible dynamics” without a separate theorem.\n\n### 3.3 Family 269: what a real robustness theorem names\n\nThe source theorem fixes all of the following:\n\n- carrier: antisymmetric `N`-particle lowest-Landau-level space on the sphere;\n- scaling: `q=3(N-1)`;\n- parent Hamiltonian: full `V_1` Haldane pseudopotential;\n- unperturbed ground space: the one-dimensional cubic Laughlin line;\n- perturbation: a sufficiently weak sum of lowest-Landau-level Toeplitz operators of a bounded real scalar potential;\n- norm: scalar-potential sup norm at most one, with a uniform amplitude threshold;\n- quantifier: sufficiently large `N`, uniformly over allowed potentials;\n- conclusion: a unique perturbed ground state and a spectral gap bounded below independently of `N`.\n\nThe repository's Lean scope is narrower: it formalizes the unperturbed spherical and Fock-space gap inequalities, not disorder stability or perturbed uniqueness.\n\nThis supplies a reusable contract for future Spin(2,3) robustness claims. It supplies no transfer theorem to paper 3 because that paper uses a two-band lattice Hamiltonian and an open-system/dephasing prescription, not a weak Hermitian scalar potential in the Laughlin lowest-Landau-level carrier.\n\n### 3.4 Two explicit separations\n\n**Topology without the proposed dynamics.** Family 151 has nontrivial homological growth with zero topological entropy.\n\n**Dynamics/response without topological change.** In paper 3, changing `gamma` leaves the closed QWZ Hamiltonian `H(k)` and hence its Hamiltonian Chern number unchanged, while the proposed response integrand is multiplied by\n\n`4 E(k)^2 / (4 E(k)^2 + gamma^2)`.\n\nFor any `E(k)>0`, this factor decreases strictly from one as `gamma` increases. Thus the response changes while the band invariant does not. This is already a countermodel to “fixed Chern number implies fixed response” within the paper's own effective model.\n\n## 4. Revised authority for papers 2 and 3\n\n### Paper 2 — DIII winding number\n\n- The explicit `S^3 -> SU(2)` map and `|W_3|=1` remain a scoped mathematical calculation.\n- The sign of `W_3` remains convention dependent.\n- Reading the massless equator as a physical transition locus remains proposal-only.\n- No dissipative law, transport coefficient, anomaly-inflow theorem, or material realization follows from the invariant alone.\n\n### Paper 3 — dissipative Chern plateaus\n\n- The closed QWZ Chern map is model-local.\n- The damping formula and doubled-cusp effect remain an effective-model derivation plus numerical prediction requiring an independent open-system derivation and experiment.\n- Family 269 does not validate this dephasing formula or its robustness.\n- The paper correctly remains independent of Spin(2,3); it cannot serve as evidence for the parent ontology without a new discriminator.\n\n## 5. Admission and next checks\n\nBefore any future claim is labelled “topologically robust”, its fact record must provide:\n\n1. carrier and state space;\n2. Hamiltonian or generator;\n3. gap notion (Hamiltonian, Liouvillian, mobility, or response gap);\n4. system-size quantifier;\n5. perturbation class and norm;\n6. threshold and preserved object;\n7. measured observable;\n8. a transfer map if the theorem originated in another model.\n\nThe next useful work is not to import more topological analogies. It is to derive the paper-3 response from the declared Lindblad generator (or exhibit the mismatch with the self-energy substitution), and separately to specify a Spin(2,3) Hamiltonian/generator on which a gap and a perturbation norm can actually be stated.\n\n## Primary sources\n\n- [Boundary-field spin-one Heisenberg-chain paper](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/paper.pdf)\n- [Spontaneous-magnetization Heisenberg paper](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/paper.pdf)\n- [`C^1` entropy-counterexample paper](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/A-C1-Counterexample-to-the-Entropy-Conjecture-September-25-2026/article.pdf)\n- [Uniform spherical Laughlin stability paper](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/uniform-stability-spherical-laughlin-gap.pdf)\n- [Family 269 Lean-scope document](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/lean/docs/269.md)\n- [Family 271 Lean-scope document](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/lean/docs/271.md)\n\n")
("v3", "value", "v4")
