("v8", "scalar", "# RIO-022-W3 — prospective d=2 neutrino preregistration\n\nStatus: `complete-prospective-test-preregistered`\nLocked: 2026-10-09, before evaluation of any later qualifying JUNO release.\n\n## Frozen hypothesis\n\nThe selected rung is `d=2`, with normal ordering and the boundary assertion `m1=0`. The observable is\n\n`r = Δm²21 / Δm²31`\n\nusing the positive atmospheric splitting under normal ordering. The point target is\n\n`r* = [1 / (φ⁴ − 1)]² = 0.029179606750063085`.\n\n## Eligible evidence and estimator\n\nThe test uses the first later public JUNO result reporting central values plus either a joint profile likelihood or covariance for the two frozen splittings under normal ordering. Prefer the collaboration-published ratio profile. Otherwise form the ratio from the published central values and propagate the joint covariance, including its covariance term. If neither is available, record `not-evaluable`; do not replace the missing dependence with independent errors.\n\n## Decision rule\n\nReject the frozen target when its one-sided lower-tail separation is at least 3σ (`p ≤ 0.00135`). If the published likelihood is asymmetric, use its lower-side profile. A result below the kill threshold only means `survives-this-release`; it is not confirmation. An absolute separation at most 1σ may be recorded as a prospective numerical hit only when every source gate passes, and still does not derive the rung or boundary assumption.\n\n## No-migration covenant\n\nAfter the release is seen, do not change the rung, ordering, lightest-mass boundary, denominator convention, target, estimator, uncertainty treatment or threshold. Do not replace the formula by a neighboring monomial. The retained `d=1` failure remains visible as a negative control. Rejection closes the physical ladder claim but preserves reproducible algebra, software and audit infrastructure.\n\nAlready-seen values are used only for power context, never for the prospective score. At the inherited central ratio `0.0300159299`, the target was `−1.78σ`; under the corpus's JUNO design-scale forecast the separation would be about `7.73σ`, with an illustrative 3σ central-ratio boundary near `0.02950418`. The actual test must recompute this boundary from the qualifying release's published uncertainty.\n")
